# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import Gate

enum GateError: Error { case closed, protocolError(String), error(String) }

struct ByteStream {
  init(read: (Int) throws(GateError) -> [UInt8],
       write: ([UInt8]) throws(GateError) -> Void, close: () -> Void)
  static func node(read: consuming NodeIPC.NodeClient,
                   write: consuming NodeIPC.NodeClient,
                   close: () -> Void) -> ByteStream
  static func channel(_ end: consuming Handle) -> ByteStream
}

enum SealedStream {
  static func initiate<Key: NoiseKey>(_ raw: ByteStream, key: Key,
      remote: [UInt8], ephemeral: [UInt8], prologue: [UInt8])
      throws(GateError) -> ByteStream
  static func respond<Key: NoiseKey>(_ raw: ByteStream, key: Key,
      ephemeral: [UInt8], prologue: [UInt8], accept: ([UInt8]) -> Bool)
      throws(GateError) -> (ByteStream, [UInt8])
}

final class NinePClient {
  init(_ stream: ByteStream, msize: UInt32 = 65560) throws(GateError)
  let version: String
  var iounit: UInt32 { get }
  func fid() -> UInt32
  func rpc(_ request: Fcall) throws(GateError) -> Fcall
  func attach(_ fid: UInt32, uname: String, aname: String)
    throws(GateError) -> Qid
  func walk(_ fid: UInt32, _ newfid: UInt32, _ names: [String])
    throws(GateError) -> [Qid]
  func open(_ fid: UInt32, mode: UInt8)
    throws(GateError) -> (qid: Qid, iounit: UInt32)
  func create(_ fid: UInt32, name: String, perm: UInt32, mode: UInt8)
    throws(GateError) -> Qid
  func read(_ fid: UInt32, offset: UInt64, count: UInt32)
    throws(GateError) -> [UInt8]
  func write(_ fid: UInt32, offset: UInt64, _ data: [UInt8])
    throws(GateError) -> UInt32
  func stat(_ fid: UInt32) throws(GateError) -> Dir
  func wstat(_ fid: UInt32, _ dir: Dir) throws(GateError)
  func clunk(_ fid: UInt32)
  func remove(_ fid: UInt32) throws(GateError)
}

final class Export {
  static func serve(_ stream: ByteStream, namespace: Namespace, root: String,
                    owner: String = "none", msize: UInt32 = 65560) -> GateError
}

final class Import {
  init(_ client: NinePClient, dispatcher: IPCDispatcher)
  func attach(aname: String = "", uname: String = "none")
    throws(GateError) -> Handle
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

These are Plan 9's exportfs and import, between Node (node(5)) and 9P2000 (9p(5)), over any byte stream.

A `ByteStream` is a connection's two directions and its hangup.
`node` makes one of a file read and written as a stream, a TCP conversation's `data` (ip(4)): reads on one channel to it and writes on another, since each may wait, and `close` what hangs it up, writing `hangup` to its `ctl`.
`channel` makes one of a channel's end; closing it closes the end.

`SealedStream` puts a Noise IK session (noise(2)) on a stream: `initiate` calls the responder whose static key is <remote>, and `respond` answers, giving the caller's static key to <accept> before it replies.
A refused caller gets no reply, and either side hangs the stream up when its handshake fails.
After the handshake, the sealed stream's bytes travel in transport messages, each framed by its length.

`NinePClient` speaks 9P2000 on a stream.
It asks for `9P2000.vx` and takes `9P2000`, and its msize is the smaller of <msize> and the server's.
Any thread may call at once: each call has a tag of its own, and a reader thread hands each reply to its caller as it comes.
An Rerror is `GateError.error` with the server's text; a stream that ends fails every call waiting, and every call after, with `closed`.
Fids are the caller's: `fid` gives a free one, and `clunk` and `remove` give it back.

`Export.serve` serves <root> of a namespace in 9P2000 on a stream until the stream ends, then hangs it up and returns why.
Each fid is a channel to its file, found by its path under <root> through the namespace, so what the namespace mounts under <root> is served, and `..` at <root> stays there.
A walk that finds its first names and not the rest is a short walk, as 9P's is.
Reads and writes are answered on threads of their own, so a read that waits, a console's, holds up nothing else; other requests are answered in order.
A directory's read gives whole stats, from offset 0 or from where the last read ended.
Node has no owners or permissions: every file is <owner>'s, a directory's mode is `DMDIR|0775` and a file's `0664`, and a wstat may change only the name, the modification time and the length.
Tauth is refused: the stream's Noise key says who is calling.
Node's errors are sent as Plan 9's strings: `file does not exist`, `permission denied`, `file already exists`, `not a directory`, `directory not empty`, `i/o error` and the rest.

`Import.attach` attaches to the server's tree <aname> and returns a channel that serves it as Node, to mount in a namespace.
Each channel opened from it is a fid of its own on the server, clunked when the channel closes.
A batch is answered on a thread of its own, its reply held until the server has answered, so a read of a remote file that waits holds up no other channel.
The server's error strings come back as Node's errors; one it doesn't know is `io`.

# EXAMPLES

Import a node's tree over an established, sealed stream, and read a file:

```swift
let gate = Import(try NinePClient(stream), dispatcher: dispatcher)
try ns.mount(try gate.attach(), at: "/n/node")
var motd = try ns.open("/n/node/lib/motd")
print(try motd.readText())
```

